28:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-the-file-abstraction",{"id":"01-the-file-abstraction","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The file: a named, persistent stream of bytes"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Strip away every convenience and a file is astonishingly simple: it is a named sequence of bytes that survives after the program that made it exits, and after the power goes off. That is the whole promise. Byte 0, byte 1, byte 2, up to some length, an array that lives on durable storage and has a name you can find it by later. Persistence is the entire point; a variable in memory vanishes when your process ends, but a file is still there tomorrow."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"To the operating system, the contents are just bytes. It attaches no meaning to them. A file holding JPEG image data and a file holding UTF-8 text are, at the file-system level, identical kinds of things: a length and a run of bytes. The interpretation: \"these bytes are a picture\", \"these are a Python script\", lives entirely in the programs that read the file, never in the file system itself. This is why you can open a .png in a text editor (and see garbage) or rename a .zip to .jpg (and confuse an image viewer): nothing checked, because there was nothing to check."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That point deserves emphasis because so many beginners believe otherwise. On Unix, a file extension is a convention among humans and applications, not a fact the kernel enforces. The kernel does not stop you renaming server.log to server.mp3, and it will not refuse to run a program because it lacks a .exe suffix, on Unix, what makes a file executable is a permission bit, which we will meet later, not its name. Tools that need to know a file’s real type inspect its leading bytes (its \"magic number\"), which is exactly what the file command does."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"File"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A named, persistent, ordered sequence of bytes stored on durable media; the OS treats the contents as opaque."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Persistence"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The defining property: a file outlives the process that created it and survives reboots, unlike in-memory data."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Extension"}],"$L2a"]}],"$L2b"]}],"$L2c","$L2d"]}],"$L2e"]}]]}],"$L2f","$L30","$L31","$L32","$L33","$L34","$L35"]}]
29:["$","$L36",null,{"prev":{"slug":"disks-ssds-scheduling","title":"Disks, SSDs & Disk Scheduling","kind":"$undefined","number":27},"next":{"slug":"file-system-implementation","title":"File-System Implementation","kind":"$undefined","number":29}}]
37:I[76156,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6238","static/chunks/app/operating-system/%5Bchapter%5D/page-6f5feff9906adfcd.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"NoteImage"]
2a:["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A suffix like .txt or .jpg, a hint to humans and applications about content type, not something the kernel enforces."}]
2b:["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Magic number"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A few identifying bytes at the start of a file that let tools like file detect its true type regardless of name."}]]}]
2c:["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"The extension is a costume; the bytes tell the truth. file reads the magic number, not the name."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ cp portrait.jpg mystery.txt      # rename a JPEG to look like text\n$ file mystery.txt\nmystery.txt: JPEG image data, JFIF standard 1.01, 1920x1080\n\n$ file /bin/ls\n/bin/ls: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, dynamically linked"}]}]]}]
2d:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Hold this picture",": "]}],"A file is a name plus a byte-stream that persists. The OS hides the disk blocks completely, and it assigns no meaning to the bytes, meaning is the reader’s job. Everything else in this chapter hangs off that one idea."]}]]}]
2e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F01-the-file-abstraction.jpg?v=8dec520c313d6162a01c8f6680ac143a","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: at the top a labelled box \"file = name + a stream of bytes\" drawn as a horizontal ruler of bytes numbered 0,1,2,…,n-1; below it a downward arrow into a grid of numbered disk blocks showing the same bytes scattered across blocks 12, 88, 5; a side note \"the OS hides the blocks\"; bottom-left a small stack of examples photo.jpg, notes.txt, a.out with a caption \"the extension is a hint, not a law\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
2f:["$","section","02-open-file-table",{"id":"02-open-file-table","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"File operations & how an fd becomes bytes on disk"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The verbs you use on a file are few and they have barely changed in fifty years: create it, open it, read from it, write to it, seek to a position, and close it. open is the pivotal one. You hand the kernel a path and say how you intend to use the file, read-only, write-only, read-write, create-if-missing, truncate, append, and if it agrees, it hands back a small non-negative integer: the file descriptor, or fd. From that moment you never mention the path again; the fd is your handle for every following read, write, and seek."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Behind that little integer stand three linked structures, and understanding them clears up a surprising amount of confusion. The fd is just an index into your process’s private file-descriptor table. Each used slot points to an entry in a system-wide open-file table, and that entry holds the two things an open file needs to remember: the access mode and the current file offset, the byte position where your next read or write will land. That open-file entry, in turn, points to the file’s inode, the single in-kernel object describing the actual file."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Why three layers instead of one? Because they capture three genuinely different things. The fd is per-process and cheap, so descriptors 0, 1, and 2 (standard input, output, and error) mean \"my input/output\", not any global file. The offset lives in the open-file entry, not the inode, so two independent opens of the same file get independent cursors, one program can be reading near the start while another reads near the end, each advancing its own position. And the inode is shared, so both see the same underlying bytes. When you read, the kernel starts at the offset, copies bytes toward your buffer, and advances the offset by the number of bytes actually transferred; lseek just moves that offset without transferring anything, which is how you jump around inside a file."}],["$","figure","3",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"open returns an fd; read/write move bytes and advance the offset; lseek repositions it; close releases it."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"c"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"#include <fcntl.h>    /* open, O_* flags */\n#include <unistd.h>  /* read, write, lseek, close */\n\nint fd = open(\"notes.txt\", O_RDONLY);   /* fd = 3, offset starts at 0 */\nif (fd < 0) { perror(\"open\"); return 1; }\n\nchar buf[64];\nssize_t n = read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);  /* reads n bytes, offset += n */\n\nlseek(fd, 0, SEEK_SET);                 /* rewind: offset back to 0 */\nn = read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);          /* reads the same bytes again */\n\nclose(fd);                              /* hand the descriptor back */"}]}]]}],"$L38","$L39"]}],"$L3a"]}]]}]
30:["$","section","03-metadata-and-inode",{"id":"03-metadata-and-inode","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"File metadata & the inode"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Every file carries a bundle of information about itself that is not part of its contents, its metadata. On a Unix file system this lives in a compact on-disk record called the inode (index node). The inode is the file, as far as the kernel is concerned: give it an inode and it knows the file’s size, who owns it, what may be done to it, when it was last touched, how many names point to it, and where on the disk its data blocks live."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Look closely at that list and notice the one thing conspicuously absent: the file’s name. The inode does not store the name. This is the single most surprising fact about Unix files, and it is the key that unlocks the next three sections. The name lives in a directory, which maps a human-readable name to an inode number. The inode holds everything about the file except what it is called, which is precisely why a file can have several names at once, or be renamed, or have its name deleted while the file itself lives on."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The inode also solves the \"where are the bytes\" problem. A small file’s data fits in a handful of blocks, and the inode holds a dozen or so direct pointers straight to them. Larger files need more pointers than fit in a fixed-size inode, so the classic design adds indirection: a single-indirect pointer aims at a block that is itself full of pointers, a double-indirect pointer aims at a block of blocks-of-pointers, and so on. That is implementation detail we unpack next chapter; here the point is simply that the inode is the map from \"this file\" to \"these scattered blocks\"."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"You can read most of the inode from the command line. stat prints it in full; ls -l is a friendlier view of the same fields. Note the three timestamps, which trip people up constantly: atime is the last time the data was read, mtime is the last time the data was modified, and ctime is the last time the inode itself changed (a permission change bumps ctime but not mtime). None of them is a \"creation time\" in the traditional sense."}],["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"stat lays out the inode fields; ls -l is the same metadata, formatted for humans."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":"$L3b"}]]}],"$L3c","$L3d"]}],"$L3e"]}]]}]
31:["$","section","04-directories",{"id":"04-directories","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Directories & path resolution"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"If the inode has no name, where do names live? In directories. And here is the elegant part: a directory is itself a file. Its contents are not free-form bytes but a structured table of directory entries, each one pairing a name with an inode number. That is all a directory fundamentally is, a lookup table from names to inodes. \"The directory contains the file\" is a convenient fiction; what it really contains is the file’s name and a pointer (the inode number) to where the file’s inode lives."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Because directories can list other directories, they nest into the familiar tree. At the very top sits a single root directory, written /, with no parent. Every file on a Unix system is reachable by starting at the root and walking down through named directories until you reach the target, that walk is called path resolution, and the kernel does it one component at a time. To resolve /home/hitesh/notes.txt, it reads the root directory to find the inode for home, reads that directory to find hitesh, reads that to find notes.txt, and lands on its inode. Each step is a directory lookup; the slashes are the joints."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A path starting with / is absolute, it is resolved from the root and means the same thing no matter where you are. A path that does not is relative, it is resolved starting from the process’s current working directory. Two special entries make relative paths work, and they exist in every directory: \".\" refers to the directory itself, and \"..\" refers to its parent. So ../config reaches up one level and then into config, and ./run.sh insists on the run.sh right here. Those two entries are real directory entries with real inode numbers, which, incidentally, is why a brand-new empty directory already has a link count of 2 (its name in its parent, plus its own \".\")."}],["$","figure","3",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Directories are files that map names to inodes. ls -i shows the inode numbers behind the names."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ ls -ai /home/hitesh\n     2 ..        81 .        96 projects   1310726 notes.txt\n#  inode#      names --------------------------->\n\n$ pwd                 # where relative paths start from\n/home/hitesh\n$ cat ./notes.txt     # \".\" = here;  resolves to inode 1310726\n$ cat ../             # \"..\" = parent (/home)"}]}]]}],"$L3f","$L40"]}],"$L41"]}]]}]
32:["$","section","05-hard-and-symbolic-links",{"id":"05-hard-and-symbolic-links","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Hard links vs symbolic links"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Now that names and inodes are separate things, a file having more than one name is not a paradox, it is just two directory entries pointing at the same inode number. That is a hard link. When you run ln a.txt b.txt, you are not copying the file; you are adding a second name for the exact same inode. Both names are equal, first-class, and indistinguishable, there is no \"original\". The inode keeps a link count of how many names point to it, and creating the hard link bumps it from 1 to 2."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This reframes what \"deleting a file\" means. The unlink system call, what rm uses, does not necessarily destroy any data. It removes one name from its directory and decrements the inode’s link count. Only when the link count hits zero (and no process still holds the file open) does the file system actually free the inode and its data blocks. So deleting b.txt while a.txt still points at the same inode simply drops the count back to 1 and leaves every byte intact. \"Delete\" is really \"remove a name; reclaim the file only when its last name is gone\"."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A symbolic link (or symlink, or soft link) is a completely different animal. It is its own tiny file, with its own inode, whose entire contents are a text string: a path to something else. It does not point at an inode; it points at a name. When path resolution hits a symlink, it reads that stored path and continues resolving from there. Create one with ln -s. Because a symlink stores a path rather than an inode, it can cross file-system boundaries and point at directories, things hard links generally cannot do, but it pays for that flexibility with fragility: delete or move the target and the symlink still exists, now pointing at nothing. That is a dangling (broken) link."}],["$","figure","3",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"ln makes a hard link (shared inode, link count rises); ln -s makes a symlink (a path pointing at a name)."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ echo \"hello\" > a.txt\n$ ln a.txt b.txt          # hard link: second name for the same inode\n$ ls -li a.txt b.txt\n1310726 -rw-r--r-- 2 hitesh hitesh 6 Jul  9 12:00 a.txt\n1310726 -rw-r--r-- 2 hitesh hitesh 6 Jul  9 12:00 b.txt\n#  ^same inode number     ^link count = 2\n\n$ ln -s a.txt link.txt    # symlink: a little file holding the path \"a.txt\"\n$ ls -li link.txt\n1310801 lrwxrwxrwx 1 hitesh hitesh 5 Jul  9 12:01 link.txt -> a.txt\n#  ^its OWN inode  ^type l = symlink            ^stored target path\n\n$ rm a.txt                # remove the original name\n$ cat b.txt               # hard link still works, data intact\nhello\n$ cat link.txt            # symlink now dangles, target is gone\ncat: link.txt: No such file or directory"}]}]]}],"$L42","$L43"]}],"$L44"]}]]}]
33:["$","section","06-permissions-and-ownership",{"id":"06-permissions-and-ownership","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Permissions & ownership"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Because a Unix system is shared, every file needs to answer \"who may do what to me\". The model is deliberately small. Each file has an owning user and an owning group, both recorded in the inode, and three classes of accessor: the owner (user), members of the file’s group, and everyone else (other). For each class there are three permission bits, read, write, and execute, giving the nine bits you see in ls -l as three rwx triads. A dash means the bit is off."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Those nine bits are almost always written in octal, because each rwx triad is exactly three bits and so maps to one octal digit: read is 4, write is 2, execute is 1, and you add them. rwx is 4+2+1 = 7; r-x is 4+0+1 = 5; r-- is 4 = 4. So the ubiquitous 755 means \"owner may read/write/execute, group and other may read/execute\", and 644 means \"owner read/write, everyone else read-only\". Once you can convert a triad to a digit in your head, the numbers chmod wants stop being magic."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The subtle part is what the bits mean on a directory, because a directory is a file of a different kind. On a directory, read means \"list the names inside\", write means \"create, rename, or delete entries\" (note: this governs whether you can delete a file, not the file’s own write bit, deletion changes the directory, not the file), and execute means \"traverse into it / use it in a path\". That last one catches everyone eventually: you can hold a valid path to a file you are allowed to read, and still be denied, because you lack execute (x) on a directory somewhere along the path and so cannot walk through it."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Two special bits round it out. Setuid on an executable makes it run with the privileges of the file’s owner rather than the user who launched it, that is how an ordinary user running /usr/bin/passwd can update the root-owned password file, briefly and under a tightly controlled program. And the sticky bit on a shared directory such as /tmp restricts deletion so that even though everyone can write there, you may only remove your own files, not other people’s. You set all of this with chmod (change mode) and change ownership with chown."}],["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"chmod sets the bits (octal or symbolic); chown sets owner and group. The special bits show as s and t."}],"$L45"]}],"$L46"]}],"$L47","$L48"]}],"$L49"]}]]}]
34:["$","section","07-mounting-and-namespace",{"id":"07-mounting-and-namespace","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Mounting & the unified namespace"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A running machine usually has more than one file system: the root disk, maybe a second drive, a USB stick, a network share. The question is how they present themselves to you. Unix makes a bold choice: there is exactly one directory tree, rooted at /, and every file system is grafted into it at some directory. That grafting is called mounting, and the directory where a file system is attached is its mount point. After you mount the USB stick at /mnt/usb, walking into /mnt/usb transparently walks into the stick’s own file system, the join is invisible in normal use. cd across it and you would never know you had crossed onto different hardware."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is a genuinely different philosophy from Windows, and the contrast is clarifying. Windows exposes each file system under its own drive letter: C:\\, D:\\, E:\\, so there are several independent roots and a path names its device up front. Unix hides the devices behind one namespace; the same absolute path always means the same place, and where the bytes physically live (which disk, which partition, even which machine for a network mount) is an administrative detail settled at mount time, not something baked into every path."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The unified tree has a beautiful consequence: not everything mounted into it has to be backed by a disk at all. Linux exposes kernel and process information through virtual file systems that generate their contents on the fly. /proc presents live process and kernel state as files, /proc/self/status describes the calling process, /proc/meminfo reports memory, and /sys exposes devices and kernel tunables the same way. These \"files\" occupy no disk blocks; reading one runs kernel code that produces the answer on demand. It is the \"everything is a file\" idea taken to its logical end: even the kernel’s own internals are browsable, greppable files."}],["$","figure","3",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"mount grafts a file system into the one tree; virtual file systems like /proc are files with no disk behind them."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/usb        # graft the USB stick onto the tree\n$ ls /mnt/usb                    # now just another directory\nphotos  backup.tar  notes.txt\n\n$ mount | grep sdb1              # see where a device is attached\n/dev/sdb1 on /mnt/usb type ext4 (rw,relatime)\n\n$ cat /proc/self/status | head -3   # a \"file\" generated by the kernel\nName:   cat\nState:  R (running)\nPid:    48213"}]}]]}],"$L4a","$L4b"]}],"$L4c"]}]]}]
35:["$","section","08-files-for-engineers",{"id":"08-files-for-engineers","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Files for engineers"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The file abstraction is clean, but real systems break in a handful of characteristic ways, and every one of them follows straight from what we have covered. Start with descriptors. They are a finite, per-process resource capped by a limit you can see with ulimit -n. Code that opens files (or sockets, sockets are file descriptors too) in a loop and forgets to close them leaks descriptors until the process hits the ceiling and every new open fails with \"too many open files\" (EMFILE). The fix is discipline: close what you open, and in higher-level languages lean on the construct that closes for you: Python’s with, Go’s defer, Java’s try-with-resources."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Next, safe writes. A tempting but dangerous pattern is to open your config file, truncate it, and write the new contents, because if the process crashes or the disk fills midway, you are left with a half-written, corrupt file and the old good version is already gone. The robust idiom exploits something we saw earlier: rename within a file system is atomic. So write the new contents to a temporary file, flush and fsync it to durable storage, then rename it over the target in one step. A reader either sees the entire old file or the entire new one, never a torn mixture. This \"write-temp-then-rename\" dance is how editors, package managers, and databases update files safely."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Two more sharp edges. When several writers share one file, the classic case is multiple processes appending to a log, opening with O_APPEND makes each write atomically seek to the true current end before writing, so lines never overwrite each other; without it, each writer trusts its own stale offset and they clobber one another. And when you truly need exclusive access, that is what file locks (flock, or fcntl byte-range locks) are for. Finally, mind the boring traps that cause real outages: text-versus-binary and newline handling differs across platforms, and paths are hostile, they contain spaces, Unicode, and \"../\" sequences, so never build a path by string-concatenation of untrusted input, or you invite a path-traversal escape out of the directory you meant to confine things to."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Which brings us to the punchline for engineers: the database you run every day is, at bottom, a small set of very large files. Postgres keeps a data directory of table and index files plus a write-ahead log; SQLite is famously a single file. The database does not get special storage from the OS, it gets the same open/read/write/fsync/rename primitives you just learned, and layers its own page structure, indexing, and crash-recovery on top of that flat byte-stream. Every durability guarantee a database makes ultimately cashes out as a well-chosen sequence of these file operations, with fsync at the critical moments to force bytes past the OS cache onto the disk."}],"$L4d","$L4e","$L4f"]}],"$L50"]}]]}]
38:["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"File descriptor (fd)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A small integer, private to a process, indexing its open-file-descriptor table; your handle to an open file."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Open-file table entry"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A system-wide record for one open instance, holding the access mode and the current byte offset."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"File offset"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The cursor: the byte position where the next read or write happens; advanced automatically, moved manually by lseek."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Standard streams"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"fds 0, 1, 2, stdin, stdout, stderr, opened for every process before your code runs."}]]}]]}]
39:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why the layering matters",": "]}],"Because the offset lives in the shared open-file entry, a descriptor duplicated by dup or inherited across fork shares one offset, so a parent and child writing to the same inherited fd take turns cleanly instead of overwriting each other. Two separate opens of the same path do not. That single distinction explains a whole category of \"why did my log get scrambled\" bugs."]}]]}]
3a:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F02-open-file-table.jpg?v=02bf01b80d9339798f9095be2dcffd52","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: three linked tables left to right. Left, \"per-process fd table\" for one process with slots 0,1,2 (stdin/out/err) and 3 pointing right. Middle, \"system-wide open-file table\" with an entry holding the current offset (offset=137) and mode O_RDONLY, an arrow from fd 3 into it. Right, the \"inode\" box with size/perms/block pointers. Below, a second process with its own fd 3 arrow into a different open-file entry that points to the SAME inode, illustrating shared file, independent offsets."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3b:["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ stat report.pdf\n  File: report.pdf\n  Size: 48213      Blocks: 96      IO Block: 4096   regular file\nDevice: 8,1        Inode: 1310726    Links: 1\nAccess: (0644/-rw-r--r--)  Uid: (1000/ hitesh)  Gid: (1000/ hitesh)\nModify: 2026-07-09 11:02:14   Change: 2026-07-09 11:02:14\n\n$ ls -l report.pdf\n-rw-r--r-- 1 hitesh hitesh 48213 Jul  9 11:02 report.pdf\n#  ^perms   ^links ^owner ^group ^size  ^mtime      ^name (from the directory)"}]
3c:["$","dl","5",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Inode"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The on-disk record holding all of a file’s metadata and the pointers to its data blocks, everything except its name."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Inode number"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The unique identifier of a file within a file system; what a directory entry actually references."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Block pointers"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The inode’s map from file to data: direct pointers for small files, single/double/triple indirect for large ones."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"atime / mtime / ctime"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Last data access, last data modification, and last inode (metadata) change, note: not a creation timestamp."}]]}]]}]
3d:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The pivot of the whole chapter",": "]}],"The inode holds everything about a file except its name; the name lives in a directory as a pointer to the inode number. Keep that separation in your head and links, renames, and deletion all suddenly make sense."]}]]}]
3e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F03-metadata-and-inode.jpg?v=7c35da97382ad5ee1dcbb5c35db9b8b0","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a large labelled \"inode\" box on the left listing its fields, inode number, file type, size in bytes, owner uid, group gid, permission bits rwx, link count, atime/mtime/ctime timestamps, and on the right a column of \"block pointers\" (12 direct pointers, then single/double/triple indirect) arrows fanning out to scattered data blocks. A pointed callout in the corner reads \"notice: no filename here!\" with an arrow to a separate directory box holding name -> inode number."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3f:["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Directory"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A file whose contents are a table of entries, each mapping a name to an inode number."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Path resolution"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Walking the tree one component at a time, doing a directory lookup at each slash, until the target inode is found."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Absolute vs relative"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"An absolute path starts at the root /; a relative path starts at the process’s current working directory."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":". and .."}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Real entries in every directory: \".\" is the directory itself, \"..\" is its parent, the machinery behind relative paths."}]]}]]}]
40:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The whole trick",": "]}],"A directory is just a file full of name → inode mappings, and the file system is that structure nested into a tree from a single root. Every path you type is resolved by walking that tree one lookup at a time."]}]]}]
41:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F04-directories.jpg?v=0f47a63d8a4d5ef16dd190f9466ac922","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: on the left a tree rooted at \"/\" branching to home, etc, bin, with /home branching to hitesh and /home/hitesh to notes.txt and a projects folder. On the right, one directory drawn open as a two-column table of entries: name column (\".\", \"..\", \"notes.txt\", \"projects\") mapped to inode-number column (81, 2, 1310726, 96). A dotted arrow traces resolving /home/hitesh/notes.txt component by component down the tree."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
42:["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Hard link"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"An additional directory entry pointing at the same inode; all names are equal, and the inode’s link count tracks how many exist."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Link count"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The number of hard links (names) to an inode; the file’s data is freed only when it reaches zero."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"unlink"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The delete operation: removes one name and decrements the link count, data is reclaimed only at zero."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Symbolic link"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A separate file whose contents are a path to another name; flexible across file systems but breaks if the target moves."}]]}]]}]
43:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The clean mental model",": "]}],"A hard link is another name for the same file (share the inode). A symlink is a signpost to a name (store a path). Deleting removes a name, not necessarily a file, the data goes only when the last hard link does."]}]]}]
44:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F05-hard-and-symbolic-links.jpg?v=c2500beba6c0406b88e48f9c34a96b16","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram split in two. Left half \"hard link\": two directory entries \"a.txt\" and \"b.txt\" both drawn with arrows pointing to the SAME inode box (link count = 2, one data block); a note \"delete one name, data survives; link count drops to 1\". Right half \"symbolic link\": a directory entry \"shortcut\" pointing to a tiny separate inode whose contents are the text string \"/home/hitesh/a.txt\", and a dotted arrow from that string re-entering path resolution toward the real file; a note \"delete the target and the symlink dangles\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
45:["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]
46:["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ chmod 755 deploy.sh     # rwx / r-x / r-x, owner full, others read+run\n$ chmod 644 config.json   # rw- / r-- / r--, owner writes, others read\n$ chmod u+x,go-w script   # symbolic form: add exec for user, drop write for group+other\n\n$ chown hitesh:developers report.pdf   # set owner=hitesh, group=developers\n\n$ ls -l /usr/bin/passwd   /tmp\n-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root  ... /usr/bin/passwd   # the s = setuid: runs as root\ndrwxrwxrwt 1 root root  ... /tmp              # the t = sticky: delete only your own"}]}]
47:["$","dl","5",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Owner / group / other"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The three accessor classes; each file has an owning user and group recorded in its inode."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"rwx bits"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Read (4), write (2), execute (1) per class, nine bits total, usually written as a three-digit octal like 755."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Directory permissions"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"On a directory: r = list names, w = add/remove entries, x = traverse into it. x is required to use it in any path."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"setuid / sticky bit"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"setuid runs a program as its owner (e.g. passwd as root); the sticky bit on /tmp lets you delete only your own files."}]]}]]}]
48:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The bit that bites",": "]}],"A \"permission denied\" you cannot explain is very often a missing execute bit on a parent directory, not on the file itself. Remember: to reach a file you must be able to traverse (x) every directory on its path."]}]]}]
49:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F06-permissions-and-ownership.jpg?v=6bca0a2f5eca6e350d477ba9910832fd","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: the ten-character ls -l string \"-rwxr-xr--\" broken out, first char = type, then three rwx triads bracketed and labelled user / group / other. Below each triad a 3-bit octal conversion (rwx=111=7, r-x=101=5, r--=100=4) assembling to \"754\". A side panel contrasts a regular file (r=read contents, w=modify, x=execute) with a directory (r=list names, w=add/remove entries, x=enter/traverse). Bottom strip notes setuid and the sticky bit with tiny examples /usr/bin/passwd and /tmp."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4a:["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Mounting"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Attaching a file system into the directory tree at a chosen directory, so its contents appear there."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Mount point"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The directory where a file system is grafted on; traversing it crosses transparently into that file system."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Unified namespace"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The Unix model of one tree under a single root /, versus Windows’ separate per-device drive letters (C:, D:)."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Virtual file system"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A mount like /proc or /sys whose files are generated by the kernel on demand and occupy no disk blocks."}]]}]]}]
4b:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Engineer’s angle",": "]}],"In containers this idea becomes a superpower: each container gets its own mounted view of the tree, so the same /app path maps to different storage per container. And when you need live system facts in a script, remember they are just files, cat /proc/meminfo beats parsing any tool’s output."]}]]}]
4c:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F07-mounting-and-namespace.jpg?v=4cc82754ac0f68f71b05a4b9722eee95","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a single tree rooted at \"/\" with branches for /home, /usr, and a highlighted /mnt/usb node where a second small tree (a USB disk's file system) is drawn grafted on at the mount point, with an arrow labelled \"mount\". A side box contrasts Unix (one tree, everything under /) with Windows (separate C:\\ and D:\\ drive-letter roots). A lower branch shows /proc and /sys drawn as clouds labelled \"virtual, generated by the kernel, not on any disk\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4d:["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"The atomic-save idiom: write to a temp file, fsync it, then rename over the target in one durable step."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"c"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"int fd = open(\"config.json.tmp\", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);\nwrite(fd, data, len);      /* write the full new contents */\nfsync(fd);                 /* force it to durable storage first */\nclose(fd);\n\nrename(\"config.json.tmp\", \"config.json\");\n/* atomic: a reader sees the whole old file or the whole new file, */\n/* never a half-written mix, even if we crash right here.        */"}]}]]}]
4e:["$","dl","5",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"fd limit / leak"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Descriptors are capped per process (ulimit -n); failing to close them leaks until open fails with EMFILE."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Atomic rename"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Renaming within a file system is all-or-nothing, so write-temp-then-rename gives crash-safe file updates."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"O_APPEND & locking"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"O_APPEND makes concurrent appends land at the true end atomically; flock/fcntl locks give exclusive access."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Databases as files"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A DB is large files (tables, indexes, WAL) using the same open/read/write/fsync/rename primitives, with structure on top."}]]}]]}]
4f:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Where we go next",": "]}],"You now own the file abstraction from the outside: bytes and names, inodes and directories, links, permissions, mounts, and the pitfalls that bite in production. The next chapter opens the box, how a real file system lays inodes, directories, and free space across the raw disk blocks from the previous chapters, and how journaling keeps it all consistent across a crash."]}]]}]
50:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch28%2F08-files-for-engineers.jpg?v=ac1fbbbd33b18def0db0d50d04d8d855","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: four labelled quadrants. Top-left \"fd leak\": a rising counter of open fds hitting a ceiling line \"ulimit -n 1024\" then a red \"EMFILE: too many open files\". Top-right \"atomic save\": arrows write to config.json.tmp then a single rename() over config.json, captioned \"rename is atomic, no half-written file\". Bottom-left \"O_APPEND\": two writers appending to one log, each write landing at the true end, no overwrite. Bottom-right \"a database is just files\": a big box \"postgres data/\" containing table files, WAL, and indexes, with a note \"its own structure inside one flat byte-stream\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
